The [code-snippets design brief](/lore/code-snippets) requires an author-facing error for each rejected snippet.

Each error carries:
- A typed error tag.
- The source document and line.
- The rejected request.
- Requested ranges and actual file length, when available.
- A repair instruction.

Invalid metadata keeps its raw values. It has no decoded request yet.

The author needs the rejected input, its location, and a repair instruction. A stack trace alone cannot supply them.

Use the [error model](/lore/error-model) to separate expected failures from defects.
Use [schema decoding](/lore/schemas-define-the-boundary) to reject invalid input at the boundary.
Use [error accumulation](/lore/accumulate-independent-errors) to report unrelated failures together.

Related reading: *Exceptional Ruby* and *Confident Ruby*, both by [Avdi Grimm](https://avdi.codes/books/).

See [the glossary](/glossary) for diagnostic context and repair instruction.
